-	<!-- custom: details below with the help of chatgpt 5 thanks -->
-	<!--
-	Bad-health scaling (plain explanation)
-
-	Terms
-	- THRESH: the cutoff where penalties begin (e.g. -300). If total bad-health (CHP) is
-	not as bad as this, there’s no penalty.
-	- RATE: how much (%) we cut per -100 CHP once past the threshold.
-	- CHP (“Cautious Health Percent”): quick net health score from nearby tiles:
-		* Jungle = -50 each, Forest = +50 each (see XML or sevopedia for updated values) (as of now they cancel 1:1).
-		* As of now, flood Plains are ignored here (their benefits outweigh the unhealth).
-
-	How it works
-	1) If CHP is above the threshold (e.g. -250 vs THRESH -300) → 0% cut.
-	2) If CHP reaches or goes past the threshold (e.g. -350 vs THRESH -300), we cut the candidate city site total value
-	proportional to how unhealthy it is:
-	- Penalty% = min(100, RATE × (|CHP| / 100))
-	- NewValue = OldValue × (100 − Penalty%) / 100
-	(All integer math to avoid division loss of precision; we cap at 100% so value never goes negative.)
-
-	Quick examples (THRESH = -300, RATE = 10)
-	- CHP = -299 → below threshold → 0% cut.
-	- CHP = -300 → 3×100 → 30% cut → keep 70% of value.
-	- CHP = -350 → ~3.5×100 → 35% cut → keep 65%.
-	- CHP = -600 → 6×100 → 60% cut → keep 40%.
-
-	Tuning
-	- START phase uses stricter THRESH / higher RATE to avoid slow, unhealthy capitals.
-	- LATER cities use a looser THRESH / lower RATE so good long-term sites aren’t over-penalized.
-	-->
